// REFERENCE COPY - NOT LIVE. Nothing reads this file.
// The live file is ~/.mozilla/firefox/wcobxzqa.dial6/user.js
//
// Kept in the repo because the Firefox Dial's performance depends on it and a
// Firefox profile is not version controlled: recreate the dial6 profile and this
// tuning is silently gone, leaving a Dial that just feels slow. install.sh does
// NOT copy it - the profile directory name is machine-specific, and writing into
// a live browser profile is not something an installer should do quietly.
//
// To apply on a new machine: copy this into the dial6 profile directory, strip
// this header, and restart that Firefox instance.

// Firefox 152 tuning for the Dials "dial6" panel profile.
// Written 2026-07-30. Delete this file to revert everything below.
//
// This profile is a PANEL, not a browser: it holds WhatsApp Web, LinkedIn and
// perhaps another social site, in one window driven by numpad 6. That changes
// which tweaks are worth making, and it is why this file is NOT a copy of the
// main profile's user.js - see "deliberately NOT set" at the bottom, which is
// the more important half of this file.
//
// Every pref below was checked against the installed Firefox 152.0.5 (present in
// libxul or in omni.ja's default pref files) before being written here, because
// a misspelled pref is silently ignored and looks exactly like a tweak that did
// not help.
//
// Sibling context: ~/Documents/personal/pc_tweaks/firefox_performance/
//   SESSION_2026-07-27_prefs_and_tab_unloading.md   the main profile's tuning
//   NEXT_STEPS_nvdec_decode.md                      why no VA-API prefs here

// ---- Video: AV1 off, for the same reason as the main profile ----
// Decode still runs on the CPU - the external monitors are wired to the dGPU so
// Firefox composites on the NVIDIA render node and the Intel iGPU cannot engage
// (established 2026-07-24) - and AV1 software decode costs more than VP9. Sites
// fall back to VP9. It matters here because LinkedIn autoplays video in-feed.
// >>> Set back to true once NVDEC decode works; the RTX 4070 decodes AV1.
user_pref("media.av1.enabled", false);

// ---- Firefox's own tracking protection, turned up ----
// NOT an ad blocker: this is built-in ETP, so there is no extension resident and
// no filter lists to parse. It blocks third-party trackers, which on LinkedIn
// are a real share of the requests and scripts a page load pays for. First-party
// content is untouched, so LinkedIn and WhatsApp Web themselves work normally.
// If a site ever misbehaves, use the shield in the URL bar to switch it off for
// that site rather than deleting these lines.
user_pref("privacy.trackingprotection.enabled", true);
user_pref("privacy.trackingprotection.socialtracking.enabled", true);

// ---- New tab page: blank ----
// The default new tab fetches sponsored top sites and story recommendations over
// the network when it opens. This profile shows the same two or three sites
// forever, so that is pure waste. false gives about:blank.
user_pref("browser.newtabpage.enabled", false);

// ---- Fewer background disk writes (default 15000 ms) ----
// Longer than the main profile's 60000, deliberately: a panel with a couple of
// pinned tabs has almost no session state worth checkpointing, so the cost of
// losing five minutes of it is zero.
user_pref("browser.sessionstore.interval", 300000);

// ---- URL bar: no network request per keystroke ----
// Nothing gets typed into this window's URL bar; it exists to hold pinned tabs.
user_pref("browser.search.suggest.enabled", false);

// ---- Telemetry and recommendation traffic off ----
// Background pings, ping files on disk, add-on recommendation fetches. Each is
// small; the point is that this profile should do nothing at all when idle.
user_pref("toolkit.telemetry.unified", false);
user_pref("datareporting.healthreport.uploadEnabled", false);
user_pref("datareporting.policy.dataSubmissionEnabled", false);
user_pref("browser.discovery.enabled", false);

// ---- Accessibility instrumentation off ----
// Firefox builds and maintains an accessibility tree whenever an AT-SPI client
// asks for one, and that costs CPU on every DOM change - which WhatsApp Web does
// constantly. Set this back to 0 if you ever need a screen reader, Orca, or any
// AT-SPI-based automation against this window.
user_pref("accessibility.force_disabled", 1);

// ---- Let the side-loaded Dark Mode extension start enabled ----
// Default 15 disables extensions dropped into the profile directory until they
// are approved in the UI. Dark Mode was copied in from the main profile on
// purpose, so it should come up enabled rather than needing a click.
user_pref("extensions.autoDisableScopes", 0);


// ============================ deliberately NOT set ===========================
//
// browser.tabs.unloadOnLowMemory / browser.low_commit_space_threshold_percent
//   The main profile sets both. Copying them here would be a BUG: unloading a
//   tab tears down WhatsApp Web's WebSocket, and it then has to reconnect and
//   re-sync the next time you look at it. Tab unloading is right for a browser
//   holding eighty tabs and wrong for a messaging panel holding two.
//
// dom.ipc.processCount
//   Tempting for memory, but Fission is on by default in 152, so content
//   processes are allocated per site and a two-site profile already keeps very
//   few. Capping the pool would trade memory for cross-site contention with no
//   measurement to justify it.
//
// Any VA-API / dmabuf / hardware-decode pref
//   Same reason as the main profile, and the same investigation: iGPU decode
//   cannot engage while the dGPU drives the monitors.
//
// browser.safebrowsing.*
//   Disabling it would save some periodic blocklist downloads - the smallest win
//   available here, and the worst trade: this is the window where links arrive
//   from other people. Phishing protection stays on.
//
// Ad blocker extensions
//   Not wanted. privacy.trackingprotection above covers the part of the problem
//   that actually costs page-load time, with nothing resident in memory.
